            <ArticleTitle>Reduction of blood viscosity following plasma exchange.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>301-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The effect of plasma exchange with plasma protein fraction on blood viscosity was determined in seven hyperlipoproteinaemic patients with coronary or peripheral vascular disease. This resulted in decreases in whole blood viscosity of 83% and 30% respectively at the lowest and highest shear rates studied, and decreases of 21% and 59% in plasma viscosity and fibrinogen. Serum cholesterol and triglyceride were reduced by 66% and 48% respectively. Sequential studies in two patients showed that blood viscosity returned to near-basal values by the 6th day. These findings suggest that plasma exchange may result in short-term enhancement of blood flow in vessels where low shear rates predominate.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
